{"Description": "HbA1c, short for glycated hemoglobin (Hemoglobin A1c), is a laboratory measurement that quantifies the proportion of hemoglobin molecules that have become irreversibly glycated in proportion to ambient blood‑glucose concentrations. Because red blood cells have an average lifespan of about 120 days, the HbA1c value reflects a person’s average blood‑glucose exposure over the preceding two to three months. Results are expressed as a percentage (e.g., 6.5 %) and may also be reported in mmol/mol units.\n\nIn diabetes care HbA1c serves as the primary biomarker for long‑term glycemic control and is routinely used as the primary efficacy endpoint in clinical research. Clinical guidelines define target thresholds to guide therapy: a general target of < 7 % is widely recommended, with more stringent goals for women who are pregnant or planning pregnancy (pre‑conception < 6.5 %, first trimester < 6.5 %, later trimesters < 6 %). Professional societies set slightly different limits—NICE ≤ 6.5 %, ISPAD ≤ 7 %, ADA ≤ 7.5 %—and an HbA1c > 8.5 % may trigger intensification of treatment. Normal values for non‑pregnant adults are typically below 5.7 %.\n\nInsulin and other diabetes‑treatment trials commonly report baseline HbA1c means around 8.6 % and end‑of‑study averages near 6.9 % for agents such as insulin glargine (Lantus®), NPH insulin, and pediatric formulations like Toujeo 300 U/mL. HbA1c is also employed to compare glycemic outcomes between products (e.g., Levemir® vs. NPH) and to evaluate delivery devices. Research consistently shows that interventions such as continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) or structured self‑monitoring of blood glucose reduce HbA1c, reflecting correlated improvements in time‑in‑range. In some comparative studies, HbA1c remains unchanged despite changes in therapeutic regimens, underscoring its role as a stable indicator of average glucose control.\n\nWhile HbA1c provides a standardized, month‑spanning gauge of average blood‑glucose exposure, it does not capture rapid glucose peaks or episodes of hypoglycemia. Consequently, it is often complemented by short‑term glucose data from CGM or self‑monitoring to give a more complete picture of glycemic variability. Pediatric registry analyses have reported that HbA1c is not associated with severe hypoglycemia rates, highlighting a limitation of the metric. Overall, HbA1c remains a key biomarker for assessing, comparing, and monitoring long‑term glycemic management across diverse patient populations and therapeutic strategies, and it is a central outcome measure in diabetes guidelines."}
