ique—placing the needle or cannula in sub‑cutaneous tissue, avoiding muscle, rotating sites, and observing appropriate dwell times—optimizes efficacy and safety.\n\nInsulin is temperature‑sensitive and must be stored under controlled conditions. Manufacturers recommend refrigeration in the middle compartment of a refrigerator, protected from heat, direct sunlight and extreme cold; short‑term room‑temperature storage is permissible only within specified limits. Exposure to temperatures outside these ranges can denature the peptide, reduce potency, and require replacement. Routine quality checks include visual inspection for clumping or discoloration, and expired, frozen or overheated insulin should never be used.\n\nAdverse reactions are generally mild (edema, sodium retention) but rare events such as angio‑edema, bronchospasm, hypotension, shock and anti‑insulin antibody development have been reported. Insulin does not cross the placental barrier, making it safe for use during pregnancy when dosed appropriately. Successful insulin therapy depends on precise dosing, correct handling, comprehensive patient education, and vigilant glucose monitoring—often aided by continuous glucose‑monitoring systems—to achieve safe and reliable blood‑glucose control for people living with diabetes."}
